Biker reacts at lightning speed and prevents worse
On Friday evening, a traffic accident occurred on the country road between Machtolsheim and Merklingen, in which a motorcyclist was only slightly injured thanks to his quick reaction.
Motorist overlooks biker
A 44-year-old female motorist wanted to turn left with her VW from Merklingen on the national road 1230 in the direction of Machtolsheim around 21.30 o'clock.
She overlooked the 24-year-old motorcyclist coming from Nellingen.
In the Collision, manual and foot brake levers fly away
The biker still tried to avoid the car, still roamed it at the rear vehicle area.
As a result of the stripping process, the hand and foot brake levers were torn off at the Suzuki and the driver was no longer able to brake.
Mentally, he steered his motorcycle into the green rifts next to the carriageway and had it rolled out there without coming down in the process.
Biker suffers leg injury
Due to the collision with the car, the biker nevertheless suffered an injury on the Beim and had to be treated in a clinic.
Mamming: Corona mass infection: 174 harvest workers infected
After a corona mass infection on a large vegetable farm in Mamming, Lower Bavaria, almost 500 people are under quarantine and are no longer allowed to leave the farm.
A security service monitors the ban on going out.
A total of 174 harvesters tested positive for the corona pathogen Sars-CoV-2 during the series investigation, as announced by the district office Dingolfing-Landau on Saturday evening.
"In order to avoid a further spread, we unfortunately have to take this step to protect the population," explained Landrat Werner Bumeder (CSU).
Previously, seven employees had already become infected in the company - this was the occasion of the series test.
The farm is a large vegetable farm that grows strawberries, cucumbers, cabbage and beetroot - but the cucumbers only for insertion jars.
Mamming is a rural community with a good 3300 inhabitants not far from Dingolfing with its large BMW plant.
Bumeder appealed to citizens to keep calm: "According to our information, it is a closed group of people".
On the yard, infected people have been separated from uninfected employees, the site is now surrounded by a barrier fence.
Greens and SPD gave co-responsibility to the state government.
The two opposition factions accused Prime Minister Markus Söder (CSU) and his cabinet of negligenting the control of the shelters of seasonal workers.
"This can take bitter revenge if such local infection events have to reach out and regional lockdowns have to be ordered," said Jürgen Mistol, the Green Party's parliamentary managing director.
"After the corona outbreak in the Wiesenhof slaughterhouse in mid-May, we have already called for a special control program also for farms with harvest helpers," said environmental expert Florian von Brunn.
"Especially the hygiene conditions at the workplace and the living conditions have to be sharply controlled from our point of view.
That obviously didn’t happen.”
The outburst had come "with announcement."
Aidenbachstraße underground station: Dangerous dispute: 19-year-old falls into track bed after shudder
In a dispute between two youth groups at Aidenbachstreay, a 19-year-old fell through a shover and slipped into the track bed.
Obersendling - On Friday there was a dispute between two youth groups at Aidenbachstray at around 16:15.
A passer-by alerted the police, who approached with several stripes.
According to initial investigations, a Munich (17) shoved a 19-year-old to the ground.
The young man slipped over the edge of the platform and fell into the track bed.
People from the 17-year-old's group helped the overthrown climb out of the track.
There was no danger from an incoming railway.
According to the polices, there were disputes between the two people again.
The 17-year-old initially fled the crime scene, but his personal details could be determined afterwards.
Accompanied by his father, he went to police inspection 29.
The 17-year-old is being investigated for assault.
Report: Seehofer pushes for prolonged data retention
Data retention is currently suspended.
According to a media report, however, Federal Minister of the Interior Horst Seehofer calls for an extension of the storage period.
He wants to strengthen the fight against child abuse.
According to a media report, German Interior Minister Horst Seehofer (CSU) calls for an extended retention of data - instead of the planned ten weeks half a year.
As the "Bild am Sonntag" reports, Seehofer Justice Minister Christine Lambrecht (SPD) asked in a letter for a quick change in the law.
Seehofer therefore wants to strengthen the measures against child abuse.
He believes it makes sense "to make urgently needed adjustments in the law already now," the newspaper quotes from the letter.
Data retention is currently suspended.
A decision of the ECJ is expected.
Seehofer, however, wants the changes in advance so that they could be applied immediately after a possible positive verdict, the report said.
A spokesperson for the Ministry of Justice told the newspaper that it could only be sensibly examined whether legal adjustments were needed if the courts had ruled.
In the fight against child abuse and child pornography, Seehofer, together with the interior ministers of the countries, had already advocated data retention in June.
NBA: Stripclub trouble for Clippers star Lou Williams
Excitement in the NBA!
Lou Williams (33), basketball pro of the Los Angeles Clippers, is said to have left the tournament quarters at Disney World in Orlando, Florida with permission for a funeral.
According to U.S. media reports, he visited the strip club "Magic City" in Atlanta in the evening.
Williams, who is said to have admitted the attendance according to ESPN data, is back in Isloation, is tested daily for Covid-19.
His reason for the visit: Chicken Wings.
In February last year, "Sweet Lou," as he's called in the NBA, already voiced his love for exactly these wings.
“Magic City and Blue Flame (another strip club in Atlanta) have the best wings in the city.”
NBA insider Shams Charania announced on Twitter on Sunday that the NBA Williams has prescribed a ten-day quarantine.
As a result, the Clippers star misses the first two official games after the Test match phase.
In Disney World, the NBA wants to end the corona season.
Currently, the best 22 teams are preparing with training matches for the season finale starting at night on Friday.
Söder comments after mass infectone on corona situation
Health Minister Melanie Huml (CSU) has already announced a test offensive for large farms on Sunday.
The press conference will be shown live on our homepage.
The case in Mammming in Dingolfing-Landau county has spooked authorities and politics.
On the large vegetable farm, almost 500 harvesters from several countries are currently working, over a third have become infected with the corona pathogen.
The farm is now under quarantine and is cordoned off with a construction fence.
No one is allowed to leave the premises, food will be delivered.
The authorities are now offering free corona tests to more than 3300 residents of the municipality.
Since the harvest workers had little contact with the local population, the district office so far assumes that the infections have not spread beyond the farm.
'Conscientious bites': Confession of suspected Nantes arsonist
"My client has shown himself cooperative," lawyer Quentin Chabert told the newspaper.
The defender added: "He very much regrets the actions, the confession was a liberation for him".
His mandate was "afflicted by remorse and greatly taken away by the extent of the destruction."
In the event of a conviction, the man, according to the prosecutor's office, faces ten years imprisonment and a fine of 150,000 euros.
The Gothic St. Peter-and-Paul Cathedral in Nantes had been severely damaged in a blaze during the night to Saturday last week.
The fire had erupted at three distant spots in the church.
Laboratory investigations substantiated suspicions of arson.
The now detained suspect had already been arrested a few hours after the fire.
He was released after one day.
The man works as a volunteer for the Diocese of Nantes and was responsible for closing the cathedral on the eve of the blaze.
Since there was no trace for a forcible entry into the church, he quickly came into the field of view of the investigators.
Discussing abolition of the 50+1 rule
Former DFB president Reinhard Grindel has recommended considering the abolition of the 50+1 rule.
"German football should at least discuss conditions and clear rules for investors before the EU Commission creates concentrated facts," Grindel told Bild am Sonntag.
He emphasized: "For the clubs that are most likely to make Bayern and Dortmund dangerous, the 50+1 rules don't work anyway: see Leipzig or Leverkusen.
In this respect, one cannot avoid the debate whether it is not necessary to facilitate the involvement of investors for associations that want to break into this phalanx."
However, he has doubts whether there is a willingness in the German Football League to allow majority stakes, which are prohibited in the Bundesliga by the 50+1 rule.
"It remains a legal risk to draw investment fences around the business location Bundesliga in a European single market," Grindel said.
The former DFB boss sees the danger that "the attempts to ensure more competitive balance in the Bundesliga in the end only weaken the competitiveness of our top clubs at European level, but do not lead to more tension in the championship".
Even an equal distribution of TV money will not achieve a decisive effect.
"FC Bayern makes a turnover of 750 million euros, Borussia Dortmund of 500 million and RB Leipzig of 300 million.
So the differences are already immensely among the first three clubs alone.
The other 15 clubs have an average household of 130 million," the 58-year-old explained.
Looking at the past, Grindel feels betrayed.
"Unfortunately, there have been individuals who have noticed this mistake and pierced on the press, rather than address me," Grindel said over accepting an expensive watch that led to his resignation in April 2019.
"That's what kept me very busy humanly."
He had received the gift worth around 11,000 euros from a Ukrainian official.
"I committed a compliance breach, and it was right to derive the consequences from this," Grindel said.
"I handled the ominous watch gift completely transparently and carelessly.
Many in the DFB knew about it.”
He had never "made a secret of the gift," the 58-year-old assured.
"You may call it naïve, in any case, this form of transparency has been the complete opposite of corruption."
"Black Lives Matter": Injured and one dead
Protests against police violence in the United States have intensified again by Donald Trump's use of federal police in several cities.
There were clashes between demonstrators and polices in various cities on Saturday.
Three people were wounded in Louisville and 16 people were arrested in Seattle.
In Austin, one person was killed by gunfire, while a demonstration train just crossed an intersection.
Previously, clashes had once again occurred in Portland during protests against racism and police violence.
The security forces, including officers of the federal police, used tear gas on the night of Saturday and forcibly drove demonstrators apart.
Hundreds, partly armed protesters marched through Louisville in Kentucky on Saturday and demanded that those responsible for the death of Breonna Taylor be held accountable.
The 26-year-old student had been shoot in March during a drug raid in her apartment in Louisville.
The aim of the raid was someone else who didn't live there at all, and no drugs were found either.
On Saturday, when a member of a group that describes itself as an armed alliance of black Americans fired a shot, three people were injured.
According to NBC, they were taken to hospital.
None of them were in danger.
Police set up barricades because counter-demonstrators from a white militia had also formed.
There was ultimately no clash between the two groups.
In Austin, Texas, a person was shooted and died in the wake of a "Black Lives Matter" demonstration.
Police took a suspect into custody, according to his own data.
She has not yet provided further details on the circumstances of the crime.
Mayor Steve Adler shared, according to the New York Times, that he, like many in the city, was "heartbroken and stunned" because of what had happened.
Also in the American metropolitan metropolis of Seattle, protesters delivered clashes with the police on Saturday.
They stopped on fire in front of a youth prison construction truck, the police used dazzling grenades and pepper spray.
According to the Seattle Times, 16 people have been arrested for resisting police or assault.
A courthouse has been set on fire in Oakland, California.
A small group of protesters smashed windows, shot down firecrackers and dazzled police officers with lasers, Oakland police said late Saturday (local time).
The fire can be extinguished quickly.
In Aurora, a suburb of Denver in Colorado, a car drove through a crowd demonstrating on a highway.
According to the police, one of the protesters fired a gun and at least one person was taken to the hospital injured.
In addition, windows were also smashed there in a court and a fire was set, the police said.
Investigations have been commenced.
Rakoczy in Bad Kissingen: Historical costumes defy corona
Although the big buzz was lacking in the spa town, visitors and performers enjoyed the Rakoczy flair of the alternative programme.
It was unfamiliar for visitors, performers and organizers: The Rakoczy weekend without Rakoczy festival.
But: Even if the big party mile in the city center was missing, the crowds and program highlights such as Saale burns, Rakoczy-Ball or pageator, many got their money’s worth in the corona-compliant alternative program.
"I think we've been able to spread Rakoczy-Flair," says spa director Sylvie Thormann.
The guests would have taken it well.
In principle, one returns to the beginnings of the Rakoczy festival, when only the historical ones were the focus of the festival.
Pleasant Atmosphere
Vesna Konhäuser and her husband have always been attending Rakoczy-Fest Bad Kissingen for more than 25 years.
So also this time in the corona year.
The couple has made themselves comfortable at Luitpold-Casino, observes the historical while strolling and listens to the Cuban live music at the casino restaurant.
"I think it's quite pleasant too," says Konhäuser.
She misses the crowds, which otherwise don't belong to Rakoczy.
But she lacks the usual program highlights, especially Saale burns on Friday evening as well as the historical pageant and the final fireworks on Sunday evening.
"It's a shame.
The weather would have been great this year," she said.
Rakoczy riding tournament to take place again
For the performers of the historic spa guests, the alternative program sometimes meant more work.
For example for Christina Gold, who participates in a normal year and can be seen there on the carriage of the spa garden café.
This year she was walking in the city on Saturdays and Sundays in costume.
"That's more exhausting, otherwise we'll have it easier," she says.
The equestrian club had less to do, even if there were individual riders in the park.
"We very much hope that the equestrian quadrille will be allowed to take place again in the coming year," says chairwoman Ulrike Waldhofen.
She announced that after several years of pause in 2021, a Rakoczy equestrian tournament is also to take place again.
28 corona cases at Spanish second-tier Fuenlabrada
The Spanish football second division CF Fuenlabrada is slipping ever deeper into the crisis because of a corona outbreak.
Since the newcomer had flown to the city of A Coruña for a match from near Madrid despite initial coronavirus cases on July 20, one low blow follows the next.
By Sunday, 28 players or team members had tested positive, one came to the hospital, the club said.
Fuenlabrada from the city of the same name south-west of Madrid still had the chance to reach the promotion playoffs so far.
Now even the relegation to the B-Group of the Second League threatens.
The match against Deportivo La Coruña last Monday was canceled at the last moment, the public prosecutor's office is investigating for a possible offence against public health, the top sports authority CSD is considering the forced relegation of the club.
Players, as well as other team members, sit in quarantine at a hotel.
"Until the sixth day of quarantine, they have received no fresh towel and no gel," the sports newspaper AS wrote.
In photos you can see how players hung underwear and club clothing in their rooms in the hotel to dry, after they apparently had to wash them themselves.
Also, the bedding was not changed for five days.
Another problem is food.
It is neither good nor sufficient, wrote "AS".
The club management, on the other hand, said they were satisfied with the hotel.
Accusations of intentionally jeopardizing public health were rejected by the club's lawyer, Javier Tebas Llanas.
They had not violated the corona rules established by the Football Association, emphasized the son of the president of the Spanish Primera Division, Javier Tebas, as the news agency Europa Press and the sports newspaper "Marca" reported.
Before departure, there had been four infected people by July 19, but they were all isolated and did not travel.
Immediately before Monday's flight to A Coruña, all players and team members had been re-tested.
When the team was already in A Coruña, "five or six" other team members were the corona test positive.
The game was canceled 60 minutes before kick-off, Tebas Llanas said.
Police have to trap horses ripped out at Dampfach
Two animals were on the road.
Police led her back to her owner.
On Sunday morning shortly before 6 a.m., a road user informed two free-roaming horses on the feeder between the junction of Donnersdorf/Steinsfeld and the Theres motorway junction.
At the arrival of the officials, the horses were already again between the feeder and the village Dampfach.
Shortly before Dampfach, the confidence of the horses could be gained by the officers, they each had a rope carried in the patrol car folded.
Cyclist Tony Martin: Career sequels imaginable
Time trial specialist Tony Martin is considering a continuation of his career.
Frankfurt/Main - Seven-time time trial world champion Tony Martin can certainly imagine continuing his cycling career.
"I'm sticking with the team right now on how and in what form it could continue," the 35-year-old told the "Frankfurter Allgemeine Sonntagszeitung."
Martin is currently employed by the Dutch team Jumbo-Visma and had considered ending his career after the coronavirus break.
Negotiations with his team are also about the number of race days, he said.
Or about other problems that would plague him as a family man, Martin said.
He used to get up after a fall, and the first thought was: "Quickly back on the wheel.
Today, as the father of a little daughter, I think: what if.
The cyclist admitted that there were certainly aspects with which he was increasingly difficult.
"But it was good for me to remember from the corona time what I have in cycling and how much fun it is for me."
The time trial specialist returns to competition during the Tour de l'Ain (5 to 9 August).
Bayern: At least four dead on Bavaria's streets this weekend
The beautiful weather attracts bikers to the streets in many places.
For at least three people, the tour with the motorcycle ends deadly.
An 18-year-old pedestrian also dies.
Schönthal - In accidents on Bavaria's roads, several, mostly young people died over the weekend.
An 18-year-old motorcyclist was captured on Saturday at Schönthal (Cham district) by a car that came to the opposite lane.
Why the car fell into the opposite lane was initially unclear.
In Upper Bavaria, a 22-year-old collided with a horse-drawn carriage on his machine on Saturday.
He was on his way downhill at Beilngries, according to police.
In a left turn, he bounced against the carriage.
His injuries were so severe that he died at the scene.
Of the twelve occupants of the carriage, one man was seriously injured and a man slightly injured, the police said.
In another fatal accident, an 18-year-old died in Bad Kötzting, Upper Palatinate.
He had settled on a road on the night of Sunday and was rolled over by a 42-year-old motorist.
According to police, the 18-year-old wanted to sleep.
The driver could no longer brake in time.
At Schönberg (Freyung-Grafenau district), a 57-year-old biker crashed head-on against a car in a right-hand curve on Saturday despite full braking.
He died at the scene of the accident.
The 57-year-old driver suffered a shock, the remaining three people in the car remained uninjured.
The safety of motorcyclists was addressed on Saturday by Bavaria's Transport Minister Kerstin Schreyer (CSU).
In a letter to the organizer of biker protests against driving bans announced for August, Gabor Kovacs, the minister referred to extensive investments such as underride protection on guardrails and the defusing of dangerous curves.
"For the safety of motorcyclists, we have thus achieved a lot in recent years," the letter said.
"The number of motorized two-wheelers killed was reduced by about 28 percent compared with 2011."
Kovac tests with Monaco against Frankfurt
Frankfurt/Main (dpa) - For football coach Niko Kovac with his new club AS Monaco there will soon be a reunion with Eintracht Frankfurt.
The eight-time French master performs on 1 August (18:00) at the Hesse, the former club of the Croatian.
For the Bundesliga, it is the first and only hardness test before the return match in the Europa League against FC Basel (August 6).
Because of the coronavirus pandemic, the encounter will be held without spectators.
"We're especially looking forward to this game.
It's always nice to come to Frankfurt and meet friends," Kovac explained.
Athletically, this is an interesting test, said the 48-year-old.
Before moving to Bayern Munich, Kovac had coached the Hessians from 2016 to 2018 and led to victory in the DFB Cup.
"A high-profile opponent such as Monaco is a good test for us with regard to the game against Basel.
Like all Frankfurters, I'm looking forward to the reunion with Niko Kovac," Eintracht coach Adi Hütter said.
The 50-year-old Austrian had succeeded the Croatian at Eintracht.
In March, the Frankfurt had lost the eighth-round first leg against Basel in their own stadium with 0:3.
Nearly 50 new infections: Hof county narrowly past lockdown
In the district of Hof, the number of known corona new infections has increased further over the weekend: from 39 on Friday and 43 on Saturday (one person has recovered and no longer falls into this statistics) to 47 on Sunday.
There's also a case in the city of Hof.
As a result, Raum Hof is approaching the critical mark of 50 new infections per 100,000 inhabitants in one week.
700 killed by monsoon rains in Southeast Asia
More than 9 million people have been affected by the severe floods in Southeast Asia, mainly in Bangladesh, India and Nepal.
Due to the heavy monsoon rain, a total of 700 people were killed.
In Bangladesh, the Red Crescent in boats strives to reach the isolated people.
Entire villages are underwater.
Farmers have lost their crops.
Tank rhinos in national park die from landslides
In Kaziranga National Park, several of the 120 tank rhinos died from landslides.
They are under protection and existing ones as endangered from extinction.
This year, the floods are particularly dire, causing scientists to rely on climate change.
Bangladesh, according to the United Nations, is one of the countries most affected by global warming.
New apartments are vacant
In Cox's Bazaar in the south of Bangladesh, the state has brought apartments for 650 displaced by the floods.
So far, only a few have moved in.
It's difficult to take people to Cox's Bazar.
Enthusiasm is also borderingled.
If you used to have a house, it was also so small and like a hut, often does not want to spend your life in a large apartment block.
Many apartments are vacant, but - as the government wants it - should soon be occupied by those who have lost their house due to the floods.
60 corona new infections in just one day in Vienna
In a 24-hour comparison, 60 new corona infections were reported in Vienna - there were 22 new infections in Lower Austria.
The Landessanitätsdirektion Wien and the medical crisis staff of the City of Vienna regularly inform about the current case numbers and other key figures on the Covid-19 virus: As of Sunday, July 26, 2020, 8.00 a.m., 4,840 (+60) positive tests are confirmed in Vienna.
The number of deaths related to the virus remains unchanged at 204.
4,090 (+48) people have recovered.
22 new coronavirus infections in Lower Austria
The number of coronavirus infections in Lower Austria has risen by 22 on Sunday to 3,288 now.
One sufferer has recovered, according to the medical staff.
This raises the number of recoveries to 2,936.
Covid-19 currently had 247 patients in the state.
147,253 tests have been carried out so far.
105 people have died in connection with the lung disease.
Argentina rejects counter-offer from creditors
The new demands could not be met, the economy ministry in Buenos Aires said on Saturday.
"This would not only be irresponsible, but also unjust," it said in a statement.
“While 50 per cent of children in Argentina live in poverty, we can’t increase the short-term profits of our creditors.”
The debt of South America's second-largest economy is no longer viable on current conditions.
That's where Argentina's private creditors are demanding to waive a portion of their claims of around US$66 billion (around 59 billion euros).
If there is no agreement, the country threatens the state bankruptcy again.
It would be the ninth default in Argentine history.
Most recently, lenders had demanded higher interest rates on government bonds and changes to some contractual clauses.
However, the Argentine government is sticking to its last offer and is nothing to make any further concessions.
Argentina is in a severe financial and economic crisis.
The inflation rate was more than 50 percent.
For the current year, experts expect economic power to decline by around ten percent.
At the end of May, Argentina had not settled US$503-million interest receivables and had thus slipped into a limited default.
Arriving professionally - private not yet
"It's going super good" - Dr. Stefanie Kramer draws this conclusion after her first days at the notary office in Eggenfelden.
In the end, everything went very fast: In mid-May, Dr. Stefanie Kramer learned that she will succeed Lutz Feller at the Eggenfelden notary office.
Already six weeks later, at the beginning of July, the graduate lawyer took up the work, the normal day-to-day business is already in full swing.
"Privately, however, I still have to arrive," she explains in an interview with the Rottaler Anzeiger and laughs.
Of course, the short lead time was not enough to relocate the center of life from Regensburg completely to Eggenfelden, reports the Dingolfinger native.
Currently, she is looking for a suitable apartment or a house in the city with her husband, who works as a judge at the regional court in Regensburg.
At least her husband was able to apply for parental leave at short notice in order to ensure the care of the two daughters (31⁄2 and 11⁄2 years old).
In order not to have to commute every day from Regensburg, she also has an overnight accommodation with her parents in Dingolfing, explains the 39-year-old.
Growing up in the neighboring district, the city of Eggenfelden has of course been familiar with it for a long time.
During on-site visits with her family in the past few months, she then again stated: "Eggenfelden is a really beautiful small town".
She was all the more pleased that she had made the race in the selection process for the vacancy.
Her native Lower Bavaria also plays a central role in Dr. Stefanie Kramer’s professional career: After studying law in Passau, she completed her legal traineeship and the 2nd state examination in Regensburg.
After several positions as a notary assessor, among others in Rehau and Hof in Upper Franconia, Babenhausen in Unterallgäu as well as Regensburg - during this time she also shouldered her doctorate in employment law - she took up her first position as a notary in Rotthalmünster in the district of Passau in 2012.
In 2016, she went on parental leave, followed by the move to Regensburg.
Since then, she has taken over representative services in various notary offices.
When the job in Eggenfelden had been advertised due to Lutz Feller's approaching retirement, she had seen the right time for the full professional re-entry, explains the 39-year-old.
The team of their predecessor, who will take over the holiday representation in the notary office in the future, has completely taken over Dr. Stefanie Kramer - and immediately has praise for the team of the law firm ready: "It's going super well!"
The fact that she can rely on well-rehearsed processes here makes it easier for her to take care of the upcoming relocation of the private center.
She has already contacted a local institution in terms of kindergarten or nursery places.
And if everything works out as desired, she hopes to be able to pursue her hobby soon - and to get to know the most beautiful jogging tracks of the Rottal.
BVB is planning for the league start with a maximum of 15,000 spectators
Dortmund The largest German football stadium in Dortmund will not even be 20 per cent filled at the start of the season in the event of a fan's return.
"Due to the distancing regulation, which is mandatory, we are planning according to the current status with 12,000 to 15,000 spectators," Borussia Dortmund's managing director Carsten Cramer told the "Tagesspiegel".
Normally, the arena in the precinct city catches just over 81,000 spectators at Bundesliga games.
In the ghost games of the past season caused by the corona pandemic, the BVB had lost around four million euros per game.
In addition to the normal spectator seats at BVB for marketing, digitization and internationalization, Cramer hopes that some of the approximately 4000 seats in the hospitality sector can be opened again: "It is of course a blow to the office if these revenues break away completely".
In view of the ongoing corona crisis, everyone is currently "well advised to take a stable side length".
"If many companies aren't doing well and 6.8 million people are in short-time work in Germany alone, then the need for entertainment is limited," Cramer explained.
The Dortmund marketing chief praised the BVB professionals for their partial salary waiver, which is intended to contribute to overcoming the crisis: "Of course, the players know that their salary waiver has a different effect than when we put 100 people working in merchandising on short-time work".
Tiny miracles: Early start to life with a few hundred grams
Hardly heavier than a packerel of butter, just three tablespoons of blood in his entire body: this was Gabriel when he was born - he weighed exactly 273 grams.
The boy is one of around 400 premature babies who are cared for annually at the Graz Children's Clinic.
A quarter of them weigh less than 1,500 grams, a few - like Gabriel and his brothers Raphael and Michael - are extreme lightweights with not even a kilogram of birth weight.
They are so small and born so early that neither the lungs nor intestines, nor muscles are fully formed.
Their skin is sometimes so tender that they could injure the electrodes of an ECG.
And they're so tiny that they're wearing dollubes at times and mini-diapers specially made for them.
But not so small that they wouldn't be in voice - "and how," smirks Berndt Urlesberger.
"Even if, of course, they don't scream quite as loudly as big babies."
When the whiteboard becomes the stage
Despite Corona Theater in the classroom: Shortly before the start of the holiday, the whiteboard as a stage experienced the 9th classes of the Sankt-Jakobus-Gymnasium in Abtsgmünd.
Actress Julia Sylvester from the Theater der Stadt Aalen presented to the pupils the production "Das Heimatkleid" - via video.
Afterwards, the students were able to exchange information about the play and the performance via Skype with the actress and the director Winfried Tobias.
The idea for the performance format from the current corona situation had been born.
Teacher Teresa Nar was happy about the enrichment of the lessons.
Even though at the end, all those involved agreed that the direct encounter in the theatre and stage room would offer additional stimulus, you can also see the potentials for contact with the schools in the theatre.
"If we continue to improve the technical framework, this could become an interesting addition to the game operation," emphasized director Winfried Tobias.
First lap drawn: The TSV 1860 would meet Eintracht Frankfurt in the DFB-Pokal
If the TSV 1860 qualify for the DFB-Pokal, the Lions will meet Eintracht Frankfurt in the first round.
This resulted in Sunday's draw.
Munich - Sixty's possible opponent in the first round of the DFB-Pokal has been determined.
The Lions would meet Eintracht Frankfurt in a successful qualifier.
This resulted in the draw on Sunday evening in the ARD sports show.
Because because of the corona pandemic, the field of participants is not yet complete, placeholders were used in the draw.
Due to the voluntary renunciation of semi-final opponent FC Memmingen, the TSV 1860 is already in the final of the Bavarian Toto Cup (4 or 5 September).
There, the Lions meet either the Würzburg Kickers or Viktoria Aschaffenburg.
If second-league champions Würzburg reached the final, Sixty would already be firmly qualified for the first round in the DFB-Pokal.
Otherwise, the Giesingers would have to prevail in the final match against Regionalligist Aschaffenburg.
The first round of the DFB-Pokal will be helded between 11 and 14 September and thus one week before the third-league start.
'Blown away from the winch': Film legend Olivia de Havilland is dead
Film legend and Oscar winner Olivia de Havilland has died.
She died on Sunday in Paris at the age of 104 of a natural death, her spokeswoman for the German Press Agency confirmed.
De Havilland was known for her role as Melanie Hamilton in the southern epic "By the Wind" (1939).
Born in Tokyo as a child of British parents, Olivia de Havilland moved to California as a toddler.
The Austrian theatre man Max Reinhardt discovered the 19-year-old in the role of Hermia of Shakespeare's "Summer Night's Dream."
Warner Brothers signed her for seven years and brought her out big alongside Eroll Flynn.
She made eight films with the heartbroker.
In 1946, she won her first Oscar in "To Each His Own."
The film ran in Germany under the title "Mutterherz".
Three years later, the accolade came as best actress in William Wyler's "The Heiress."
She also received much recognition as a neurotic eccentrist in "Der dunkle Spiegel" and "Die Schlangengrube".
Daphne du Maurier proposed her in 1952 for the lead role in the film adaptation of her novel "My Cousin Rachel."
The play became a worldwide success.
With her longtime friend Bette Davis, she starred in "Lullaby for a Corpse."
From the marriage to the writer Marcus Goodrich (1946 to 1953) the son Benjamin emerged.
In Paris, de Havilland married journalist Pierre Paul Galante and daughter Gis�èle had with him.
This marriage also shattered.
Rumours of an affair with former British Prime Minister Edward Heath, meanwhile, have never been confirmed.
"Road to Timmendorf": Beach volleyball: Thole/Wickler miss finale on comeback
World Cup runner-up Julius Thole and Clemens Wickler have missed out on the finals at the beach volleyball tournament "Road to Timmendorf."
At the meeting of the top teams in Düsseldorf in the semi-finals, they defeated their national team colleague Nils Ehlers and Lars Flüggen with 1:2 (17:21, 21:17, 19:21).
Thole/Wickler had most recently played at the World Tour Final in Rome in September of last year.
In the final match on the Rhine, their conquerors Ehlers/Flüggen against the surprise team Lukas Pfretschner and his interim partner Steven van de Velde conceded a defeat with 0:2 (17:21, 17:21).
In addition to the show tournament, three starting places for the German championships at the beginning of September in Timmendorfer Strand were played: The tickets were secured by Armin Dollinger/Simon Kulzer, Manuel Harms/Richard Peemüller and Simon Pfretschner/Milan Sievers.
Kalbitz ejection from AfD confirmed - FDP speaks of "fig sheet"
The exclusion of Andreas Kalbitz from the AfD is, from the point of view of the FDP, only a "fig leaf" and does not change the character of the party.
"The AfD is the parliamentary arm of right-wing extremists in Germany.
Andreas Kalbitz is only part of the tip of the iceberg of radicals in the AfD," FDP interior politician Benjamin Strasser told the dpa news agency in Berlin.
After several hours of trial, the Federal Arbitration Court of the AfD on Saturday in Stuttgart had confirmed the exclusion of Kalbitz, which the Federal Executive Board had decided by a majority in May.
Kalbitz is chairman of the AfD faction in the Brandenburg state parliament.
"If the AfD were really about consistently removing right-wing extremists from their party, officials like Björn Höcke as well as his supporters would have been ruled out for a long time," Strasser said.
"However, there is no political will for this in the AfD, because right-wing extremism now belongs to the party."
CSU Secretary-General Markus Blume commented similarly: "That Andreas Kalbitz now remains excluded says nothing and does not change anything," he told the "Welt am Sonntag".
"Rather, there are volumes of how many frenetic supporters a right-wing extremist in the middle of the AfD still has."
Blume assumes that the AfD will end up below five percent in elections in the future.
"The days of the AfD in the parliaments are numbered."
Kalbitz was one of the spokesperson of the now officially dissolved "wing" around the Thuringian AfD state and parliamentary group chairman Björn Höcke; the constitutional protection classifies the roar as a "secured right-wing extremist aspiration".
In May, the AfD federal boarder Kalbitz had thrown out of the party.
As a reason, he stated that Kalbitz did not specify a previous membership in the now banned far-right "Heimattreue Deutsche Jugend" (HDJ) (HDJ) and among the Republicans.
Kalbitz denies membership in the HDJ.
The leader of the left faction in the Brandenburg state parliament, Sebastian Walter, also emphasized that the decision of the Federal Arbitration Court did not change the character of the AfD: "The wolf has only eaten chalk.
The AfD remains a far-right party, whether with or without Kalbitz."
With this decision, the federal party will not be weakened nor the right wing.
"The manoeuvre of the Meuthen force is manageable.
You want to avoid further observation by the constitutional protection and try to get through under the radar," Walter said.
"The AfD doesn't get rid of the spirits she called.
An ejection doesn't change anything yet," commented the Brandenburg SPD Secretary-General Erik Stohn the decision in the Kalbitz case.
"Here, the AfD state association and the faction held to their right-wing leader until today."
The legal dispute over Kalbitz's membership in the AfD will continue.
He announced on Saturday that he would take civil action against the decision of the Federal Arbitration Court.
North Sea wind farms deliver a good 21 percent more electricity
In the first six months of the year, wind power plants on the German North Sea fed 11.51 terawatt-hours of electricity into the grid.
This is a good 21 percent more than in the same period of the previous year, said network operator Tennet in Bayreuth.
Tennet is responsible for the transmission of the North Sea electricity to the mainland and the transmission through the German power grid.
The wind turbines on the Baltic Sea, which do not belong to Tennet's grid area, have fed in a further 2.22 terawatt hours (TWh) of electricity.
In total, the wind power from the North Sea and the Baltic Sea comes to 13.73 TWh, compared to a good 11.64 TWh in the first half of 2019.
The wind harvest at sea thus accounts for almost 19 percent of total wind power in Germany; this is about four percentage points more than a year ago.
New wind farms ensure higher generation capacities
In addition to wind conditions from higher generation capacities, the increased contribution of offshore wind energy results because new wind farms have been connected to the grid in the past twelve months.
If the 1270 wind power plants in the North Sea were all running at full force at the same time, they could generate 6.7 gigawatts of electricity.
The transmission capacity is sufficient for this; according to Tennet, it is 7.1 gigawatts.
By 2030, it is expected to rise to 17 gigawatts in the German North Sea.
"In Germany and in the Netherlands, we are investing 20 billion euros in the expansion of offshore network connections by then," Tennet managing director Tim Meyerjürgens said.
In reality, wind farms cannot exhaust their capacity even at sea, but reach about 40 percent.
The previous record day this year is January 2, when the North Sea wind farms generated more than six gigawatts of electricity.
On average, the North Sea wind turbines produce about 2.5 to 3.0 gigawatts of electricity, the equivalent of three to four nuclear power plants.
Almost seven million households could be supplied with electricity.
Kunst "Skirmish" - an exhibition by the Leipzig-based artist Christian Bold in the gallery of the Gmünder Kunstverein.
This was almost a small anniversary celebration for the 130.
Birthday of the Gmünder Kunstverein: The exhibition opening "Skirmish" with works by the Leipzig artist Christian Bold blew up the usual framework after the corona break.
Much more art lovers than usual followed the dialogue between the artist and the chairman of the Kunstverein, Professor Dr. Klaus Ripper.
The musical equivalent was provided by the band "Defenders of the Universe" on the southern Kornhausplatz.
North of the gallery, the "Sandtogether" association got involved.
Freital: Windbergfest cancelled due to corona
The Windberfest will not take place this year.
The city of Freital has canceled the three-day festival planned in September.
In other years, over 30,000 people had attended this city festival.
"We really did not make this decision easy for ourselves and - knowing how difficult it is economically for those involved - hoped until the last minute," explained Lord Mayor Uwe Rumberg (non-partisan).
"But in the end we are responsible for the health of many thousands of people here as organizers."
Because if tens of thousands had flocked to the festival grounds and into the marquee, minimum distances, hygiene regulations and contact tracing according to the current corona protection ordinance could not have been observed.
Or the restrictions would have been so strong that not much would have been left of a folk festival.
In addition, the organizational and monitoring effort would disproportionately increase many times over.
Rumberg consoled for next year.
Then the city of Freital celebrates its 100th anniversary, among other things with the Winterbergfest from 7 to 9 May 2021 as well as the Day of the Saxons from 10 to 12 September 2021.
Dressage Olympic champion Werth in Achleiten unbeatable
Achleiten - The six-time Olympic champion Isabell Werth was the outstanding rider at the dressage tournament in Achleiten, Austria.
The 51-year-old from Rheinberg came to four victories over the weekend at four starts.
After she had won a Grand Prix with Weihegold and Quantaz, she was also unbeaten on Sunday in the Grand Prix Special and in the freestyle.
On the 15-year-old mare Weihegold, Werth clearly prevailed against the Austrian Victoria Max-Theurer on Abegglen on Sunday.
Also in the freestyle, the German was clearly ahead of her 34-year-old student Victoria Max-Theurer on Saturday on Quantaz.
Werth's winning horse Quantaz belongs to Max-Theurer.
The world's most successful dressage rider is expected to establish ten-year stallion in the Grand Prix.
For Quantaz, it was the first freestyle ever.
Already at the first tournament in Achleiten three weeks ago, Werth had won all the important four trials.
The last of the three international tournaments is scheduled there for August 14-16.
More air traffic controllers in Worms in the future
Worm's university continues into the air: The "DFS Deutsche air traffic control system" sponsors a professorship for "Air Traffic Management".
A corresponding contract between the university and the DFS has now been signed in Worms.
The contract was initially concluded for three years and starts in 2021.
With the professorship, it is possible to make it possible to start your studies not only in the summer, but also in the winter semester.
This allows twice as many students to be trained as before.
At the same time, the capacities in both the university as well as at DFS air traffic control academy are evenly utilized.
DFS will advise the university on the establishment and composition of the professorship.
Represented at the university since 2019
Since the summer semester 2019, the first students have been working as part of their training as air traffic controllers at Worms University of Applied Sciences.
Nine students have already started this year.
So far the experience of the DFS is very positive, the performance of the students is excellent.
The combination of studies and pilot training contributes significantly to further increasing the attractiveness of the pilot profession.
"In these times, it is not easy for us to promote economically.
However, we firmly expect air traffic to recover and grow again in the long term.
In order to be able to fulfill our legal mandate in the future, we need highly qualified personnel," says Professor Klaus-Dieter Scheurle, CEO of DFS when signing the contract.
"For DFS, the endowed professorship is a future-protective investment that will provide air traffic controllers with a broad, overarching knowledge base in the long term.
We therefore continue the good cooperation with the university and at the same time underline our attractiveness as an employer."
This is also emphasized by Professor Dr. Jens Hermsdorf, President of Worms University of Applied Sciences: "The endowed professorship forms an important bridge between science and business.
With it, we would like to build on the already existing cooperation with DFS Deutsche air traffic control as a practical partner for the dual bachelor’s degree program Air Traffic Management."
The trust of German air traffic control is at the same time motivation and obligation for Worms University of Applied Sciences to further expand its teaching and research competence in the future fields of air transport.
In use for the air traffic controllers of tomorrow, the university can set very special highlights and innovative priorities for its students with the first endowed professorship for a dual course of study.
Dr. Denis Alt, State Secretary at the Ministry of Science, Continuing Education and Culture, emphasizes: "Worms University of Applied Sciences, with its focus on air transport, has a unique selling point in Rhineland-Palatinate.
Today's signing of the cooperation agreement strengthens the profile of the university."
Rescue helicopter lands on the A 45
For about 30 minutes, the motorway A 45 was fully closed on Saturday noon in the direction of Aschaffenburg, as the rescue helicopter was in use after a traffic accident.
A 76-year-old drove with a Ford around 12.40 clock in the exit Alzenau-Mitte in the curve straight and landed at the guardrail.
As a result of the impact, the front airbags triggered, the driver suffered minor injuries to his face and had to be taken to a hospital for treatment.
The 72-year-old passenger's injuries were taken care of by the emergency doctor and the patient was subsequently flown to a hospital by rescue helicopter.
Currently, however, the police assume that there is no danger to life.
The damaged vehicle, on which a damage of about 2000 euros was incurred, had to be towed.
Heil wants to scrutinize other industries after meat industry
The corona crisis brought the conditions in parts of the meat industry to light again.
Tightening of the law is imminent.
But are they right - and will it stick with it?
Federal Labour Minister Hubertus Heil (SPD) wants to review other industries according to the now planned strict regulations against grievances in the meat industry.
"We will look at the industry for the industry and then take suitable measures for the respective industry if necessary," Heil told the German Press Agency in Berlin.
Germany's employers are already warning against restricting work contracts generally.
After the mass corona outbreak at meat giant Tönnies in North Rhine-Westphalia, the Federal Cabinet wants to pass a law of Heil this Wednesday.
Large-slaughters should no longer be allowed to use external personnel for slaughtering, dismantling and meat
For this purpose, the use of work contract and temporary workers should be prohibited there.
Employer President Ingo Kramer told the dpa: "Fingers away from a general restriction of work contracts and temporary employment if you don't want to strangle the economy completely".
Work contracts are necessarily the basis for their added value at the customer, for example, for wide areas of craftsmanship, the construction industry and plant construction.
"The misconduct of individuals cannot destroy such an important instrument."
Heil assured: "My goal is not to ban work contracts everywhere in the German economy".
Work contracts make sense.
"If an industrial company instructs a craftsman to install a security system, this is a normal work contract."
The German meat industry considers a ban on work contracts and temporary agency work to be unconstitutional in its industry alone.
It is not possible to explain why other employment law should apply to portioning and packaging of cheese in the future than Wurst, according to a statement on the legislative project, as the "Neue Osnabrücker Zeitung" (Saturday) reports.
In the case of a ban, the consumer price would therefore increase by 10 to 20 percent per kilo and product.
According to the results of an audit by the NRW Occupational Health and Safety Administration, Eastern European employees from work contract companies often worked 16 hours a day in the industry.
They often didn't have a break.
Wage has been withheld for protective equipment or renting.
It was also noted that the situation in the accommodation of employees was often miserable.
Heil said that a relatively sharp law had already come into force for the meat industry in 2017.
However, these were circumvented by "tricking constructions".
"Now it has to be fundamentally cleaned up."
The politician announced: "In the Occupational Health and Safety Act, which we want to adopt in the cabinet at the end of July, there are also measures that not only affect the meat industry - for example, higher testing rates on occupational health and safety or clear criteria for communal accommodation".
The dpa draft includes amendments to the Act on Safeguarding Workers’ Rights in the Meat Industry as well as the Occupational Safety and Health Act.
Brandenburg's Consumer Protection Minister Ursula Nonnemacher (Greens) thinks more intensive checks are necessary.
"I would like us to be able to do even more checks in many supervisory areas: food inspections, animal welfare, everything possible, for example also animal transport," Nonnemacher said on Sunday in the RBB broadcast "Brandenburg aktuell".
But for more checks, more personnel positions are also needed.
Three dead in crash of ultra-light aircraft in Germany
Three people have died in the crash of an ultra-light aircraft onto an apartment building in Wesel in the German state of North Rhine-Westphalia, according to the police.
One child was slightly injured.
Two other people were rescued injured in the accident, a spokesman for the district control center Wesel said.
A parachute had been found at the scene of the accident, according to the police.
Rheinische Post, citing eyewitnesses, reported that the plane had collided with a hot air balloon before the crash.
The house is a one- or two-family house.
Further details were not disclosed at first.
Eviction of Frankfurter Opernplatz proceeded peacefully
A week after the riots in Frankfurt, the eviction of Opernplatz at night on Sunday also took place peacefully.
Finally, a few dozen people were staying in the square, but left it at 1 a.m. before the curfew began.
Police had been on duty since the early evening with numerous officers, a larger police line-up could be seen across the citizen area.
For the first time this weekend from midnight, an entry ban for the Opera Square, where riots occurred a week ago.
Polices had been attacked with bottles from the crowd of revelers.
At least five officers were injured, according to police inboxes, with several police vehicles damaged.
The night of Saturday had also remained quiet.
Car overturns: Young woman seriously injured in accident on Bundesstraße 83 near Hofgeismar
In an accident between Schöneberg and Hümme, a 21-year-old suffered serious injuries on Sunday at about 13.25.
She was taken to Kassel Hospital.
With thermal cameras, the fire brigade searched possible passengers.
According to police, the woman was travelling on Bundesstraße 83 in the direction of her place of residence Hümme, when her car came off the road from previously unexplained cause and overturned several times.
In order to save the accident, the fire department had to clear the roof of the car.
Since it was unclear whether there was still someone in the vehicle, the adjoining corn field was searched from the turntable ladder with a thermal imaging camera.
The drone squadron from Warburg, which also works with thermal imaging cameras, had also been ordered for the search for people.
However, the emergency services did not find anyone between the tall corn plants.
The seriously injured was taken by ambulance to the Kasseler Klinikum for further care.
Nearly 50 firefighters from Hofgeismar, Hümme and Schöneberg were on site.
The highway was closed for two hours because of the accident.
Juventus Turin champion in Italy
Turin - After a redemptive goal from Cristiano Ronaldo, record champion Juventus has secured the Italian championship for the ninth time in series.
The team around the five-time world footballer made the 36th Scudetto in the club's history with a 2:0 (1:0) on Sunday night in the home game against Sampdoria Genoa perfect.
Ronaldo scored at 1:0 before half-time.
With 83 points, Turin, which competed without the last long-injured ex-national player Sami Khedira, two matchdays before the end of Serie A's season can no longer be displaced from first place.
Second-placed Inter Milan is seven points behind.
Juventus were harmless despite optical superiority in the first half, then Ronaldo scored after a free-kick variant with the last action.
The 35-year-old exploited a clearance from Miralem Pjanic from the penalty area boundary directly with a flat shot (45.+7).
Against the now more offensive guests, Ronaldo was also involved in the 2:0, after his shot Federico Bernardeschi (67th) dusted off.
Thus, the lacklustre but decisive success was assured.
The foul penalty in the 89th minute, which was scored at the slats, is likely to have been properly scored by Ronaldo himself with regard to the goalscoring standings.
"It's complicated to stay mentally and physically at ball level for 90 minutes.
The games are strange, and the momentum changes very easily," coach Maurizio Sarri explained after the 1:2 at Udinese Calcio on Thursday.
Above all, the mental exhaustion of his players is great, the Juve coach said.
Due to the defeat, the Turins had not yet been able to make the championship perfect.
Juve owed the reassuring advantage at the top of the standings, especially to the weakness of the competitors.
Because in the eleven games before Sunday's game, the Sarri squad had managed only five victories.
Whether Ronaldo will also become the top scorer is questionable.
While the Portuguese superstar has scored 31 goals so far, the former Dortmund Ciro Immobile has after his three-pack at the 5:1 (1:1) of the table fourth Lazio Rome at Hellas Verona on his account - as many as Robert Lewandowski contributed to FC Bayern's title win.
The record in Italy is held by the current Juve professional Gonzalo Higuaín.
The Argentine scored 36 goals for the SSC Naples in the 2015/2016 season.
In August, the "Old Lady" of Italian football awaits the next title chance.
Juventus are in the eighth final of the Champions League, but have to make up for a 0:1 first-leg break against Olympique Lyon on August 7 in order to reach the final tournament in Lisbon.
In the next knockout round, Real Madrid or Manchester City would wait as opponents.
Baden-Württemberg: More than 250 illegal car races recorded
Despite all threatened punishments and court rulings, motorists continue to gas in countless illegal races on the Baden-Württemberg roads: Last year and the first four months of this year, the police in the southwest have already registered more than 250 illegal car races.
There are no comparative values for the years before, but the razors are increasingly working for the judicial authorities: A total of 155 investigative proceedings were conducted at the public prosecutor’s offices between Konstanz and Mannheim in 2018 because of illegal races, one year later there were already 252 and in the current year until the beginning of June already 151.
The races were less about organized events, as can be seen from the Interior Ministry's response to a request from the Landtag SPD.
In most cases, the drivers spontaneously competed against each other or they took flight from the police - this is also indicated as an illegal race.
German men aged between 18 and 60 were at the wheel.
113 fines - 15 times imprisonment
Only in comparatively few cases, the razors are caught and also asked to checkout: According to the Ministry of the Interior, a total of 113 fines have been imposed and 15 imprisonment sentences have been issued since 2018.
However, there is no indication that the number of illegal car races is actually increasing.
Rather, other motorists reacted more sensitively because of the several tragic accidents and trials of previous years than before and indicated more frequently, a ministry spokesman said.
Particularly popular with razors is the Autobahn 8. Again and again, motorists slow down the traffic there in order to gain space and use the free distance for a race.
Last August, a dozen drivers were caught near Kirchheim unter Teck, three months earlier there were even 18 drivers on the A8 at Merklingen.
A81 is the most popular "racing track"
On the A81, the police even register more races than on any other federal highway.
With a speed limit, an attempt is made there to slow down the razors on the particularly affected section between Engen and Geisingen.
The mostly high-priced and upgraded sports and luxury bodies often have Swiss license plates, because the drivers dodge to Germany because of the high penalties for too fast driving and car racing in the neighboring country.
For the SPD, the speed limit must not be reduced to a few routes: "With a general speed limit of 130 kilometers per hour, we would overcome this problem on highways," says Martin Rivoir, the traffic expert of the SPD parliamentary group.
"The inhibition threshold for razors would be significantly higher if you should not be legally on the road here and there with 200 kilometers per hour and more."
Rivoir thinks less of enlightenment or image campaigns: "If you override all rules in such a way and jeopardize not only your own life, but also the lives of other road users, you are hardly reachable with reason".
ADAC calls for stronger police crackdown
This is not enough for the ADAC: "We assume that razors cannot be deterred by the speed limits," says Holger Bach, Head of Transport and the Environment at ADAC Württemberg.
He calls for additional and also tougher checks on the affected routes.
It is not uncommon to become more painful and consequential seriousness from the high-turrout struggle: Since January 2019 and until April 2020, three people have died at the races nationwide, two of them uninvolved in a nocturnal crash in Stuttgart’s city centre and one in Gerlingen.
Two young men are said to have delivered a car race there in November.
One of the vehicles came off the road, bounced against a garden wall and was left lying on the side, the 18-year-old passenger died.
21 other people were injured in the races of the past two years.
According to Interior Minister Thomas Strobl, stricter penalties are not necessary: "Legislative action in order to sanction illegal motor vehicle races more harshly is not seen," according to the Interior Ministry paper.
July 23 blue light: Thirsty burglar steals drinks from takeaway
Rohrdorf: A man broke into a takeaway on Thursday in Rohrdorf (Calw district) and stole drinks and cash.
A witness heared noises in the valley street around 3.40 a.m. and ran towards the takeaway.
There he noticed a man who served himself on the refrigerator with drinks and left the snack afterwards through a previously smashed front door.
The perpetrator fled in the direction of the meadows behind the takeaway.
The burglar, according to police, also used a checkout and stole cash in the low three-digit range.
The suspect is described as follows: He is about 1.75 to 1.80 meters tall and of strong stature.
In fact, he wore a black hooded sweater, black pants and bright shoes.
The police are taking further witness information.
Ackermann sprints to victory on final stage at Sibiu Tour
Cyclist Pascal Ackermann won his second victory of the day on the closing stage of the 10th Sibiu Tour.
The 26-year-old Palatinate from Team Bora-hansgrohe prevailed on the 109-kilometre section with start and finish in Sibiu in the mass sprint, having already won a stage on Saturday.
It was season victories three and four for Ackermann.
In the overall standings, his Austrian teammate Gregor Mühlberger prevailed.
The 26-year-old had confidently won the mountain time trial in the morning and thus took over the top in the classification.
Only 104 riders had competed in the evening for the final stage.
No fewer than 18 starters had fallen victim to the time limit in the mountain time trial.
"I'm really happy to start the season restart with a win," Ackermann had declared after his success on Saturday.
"The sprint was really hectic," the 26-year-old said.
300 meters before the goal, he simply put on his sprint, "and it worked out perfectly".
According to the rules of the world governing body UCI, a corona test was taken for all race participants and their companions six days before the start of the race.
During the complete round trip, drivers and caregivers were separated from the organizing team as well as from the media in isolated hygiene bubbles.
Spectators at the start and finish were not allowed.
Control center gave instruction on lay resuscitation
Düsseldorf - Tragic accident on Sunday noon in Düsseldorf-Garath.
In an apartment building, a child (4) suddenly lay lifeless in the water while bathing.
According to the fire department, the emergency call was received at just before 3 p.m.
"A control center dispatcher immediately gave instructions on lay resuscitation," a fire department spokesman told BILD.
The incoming rescue workers took over the resuscitation measures a little later.
The child, who is in danger of death, was transported to the university hospital under resuscitation.
Why it crashed is unclear.
Police have routinely commenced investigations into the accident.
The fire spokesman: "We assume a tragic accident in the apartment".
Nuremberg: Unapproved tuning meeting - police intervene, 700 vehicles and around 1100 people
Hundreds of cars and temporarily over 1000 people took part in a "tuning meeting" in the parking lot of a furniture store on Saturday night.
Police implemented their announcement that increased control of such events.
There was a big tuning meeting on Saturday evening (July 25, 2020) in Nürnberger Südstadt.
Hundreds of vehicles were involved.
According to the Middle Franconia police, an increased number of vehicles in the area of a large parking lot in Ingolstädter Straße had been detected around 22.30 o'clock by the Nuremberg traffic police.
As a result, the police carried out a large number of vehicle checks there.
Only recently, the police had announced that they wanted to take greater action against so-called autoposers.
Illegal tuning meeting in Nuremberg: Only small part from the "scene"
The officers counted about 700 vehicles and about 1100 people, the police said.
Of this, however, only a small part of those present can be assigned to the "autoposer" and tuning scene.
Apparently, the majority of the relevant scene complied with the appeal of the police.
On the basis of the vehicles and persons on site, it can be assumed that these were predominantly onlookers.
In addition, the predominant share of those present adhered to the applicable infection protection regulations, in particular compliance with the minimum distance and the wearing of mouth-nose protection.
The on-site control measures were completed around 1:00 p.m.
In totality, the officials found such structural changes for 10 vehicles that the operating permit had expired.
Four of these vehicles had to be secured.
No car races
There weren’t any illegal car races.
"The Middle Franconian Police Headquarters will continue to keep an eye on what is happening and will continue to carry out increased control measures, including in connection with the advent of onlookers," it said.
The agency News5 spoke with Jürgen Schrödinger of the towing company involved: "They all have beautiful rifts, which we must not scratch," says Schrödinger.
Special tapes would be used for this.
Also because the cars are wider, the company has to take care not to cause any damage.
"After all, the young people should get their cars back unscathed."
Employment agency figures: 900 suspected cases of short-time work abuse
Berlin - The Federal Employment Agency (BA), according to its own statement, investigates around 900 suspected cases of abuse in short-time work allowances.
"That's extremely little for more than 880,000 companies that have reported short-time work," Daniel Terzenbach, who is responsible for operational business, told the Frankfurter Allgemeine Zeitung newspaper.
At the beginning of the corona crisis, at the beginning of the corona crisis, a large number of companies suddenly applied for short-time work, BA had to react quickly.
Normally, the exam takes up to three weeks.
"So much time was not available to us at this hot phase.
All the more precisely we will look at the final statements.
I'm sure there's going to be a lot more.
We'ren't naïve and we've got a long breath."
Evidence of abuse would often come from the affected employees or unions.
In addition, BA uses analysis software to detect abuse patterns.
According to Terzenbach, BA is preparing for a possible wave of bankruptcy in the fall.
"Will there be significantly more bankruptcies than in the previous year?
The assessments diverge, but we prepare ourselves in the event and are already training our employees in the employment agencies to process applications for bankruptcy money."
The numbers are still inconspicuous, Terzenbach said.
World Heritage in Potsdam: Party dissolved in the New Garden
Potsdam - On Friday evening, about 40 teenagers celebrated a party in the New Garden.
The collection was dissolved at 20.48 by the precinct drivers of the Prussian Palaces and Gardens Foundation (SPSG), the foundation announced at PNN's request.
The teenagers then left the park.
On Saturday evening, some teenagers would have played rugby in the New Garden.
The meadow had been cleared by precinct drivers, SPSG spokesman Frank Kallensee said on Sunday.
Time and again, there are illegal celebrations in the parks
In the parks of the foundation, there are always accumulations in which the distancing and hygiene rules Covid-1-19 bypass ordinance are not complied with.
At the end of June there was a celebration with about 300 people in the Park Babelsberg, a week later about 150 people celebrated in the New Garden.
Even without coronavirus ordinance, the parks are closed at nightfall.
Storage in the meadows is prohibited according to park regulations, there are only a few exceptions.
For a long time, the foundation has complained that visitors would leave more and more rubbish and destroy protected biotopes.
In Berlin, meanwhile, illegal park parties have reached a different dimension.
In the Hasenheide in the district of Neukölln 5000 people celebrated an illegal techno party on Sunday night.
The event has been disbanded by police.
Austria: Mass testing after coronavirus outbreak in tourist resort
With hundreds of tests, authorities have reacted to a corona outbreak in the popular Upper Austrian tourist resort of St Wolfgang.
The number of confirmed cases rose to 48, according to the state authorities in Linz.
More than 100 results were left in the evening.
Accordingly, many of the infected are young interns in tourism companies, but at least one holiday guest also tested positive.
Residents, guests and tourism staff were asked to stay in their shelters on Saturday night.
The curfew was set at 2pm.
"There are cancellations and cancellations there and there" of bookings, the head of the tourism association Wolfgangsee, Hans Wieser, told the dpa.
There have also been occasional guests leaving early.
St. Wolfgang is the most important tourist resort in Upper Austria, with more than 400,000 overnight stays last year.
In a normal season, around a third of the guests are Germans.
On Friday, the first infections had become publicly known.
The head of the district authority, Alois Lanz, on Saturday defended the behavior of the interns, who had celebrated in several venues and may have become infected there.
"We were all happy to do this in our youth," he told Ö1 radio station.
However, two bars were closed on Friday to prevent further infections.
The tourist board launched an information campaign to sensitize young employees to the dangers of Covid-19.
From Saturday, hundreds of employees, holidaymakers and locals were tested.
In addition, all guests who were in St. Wolfgang from July 15 were contacted.
The outbreak evokes memories of the Austrian ski and party resort of Ischgl in Tyrol, which was a center of corona spread throughout Europe in the initial phase of the pandemic.
In total, more than 1500 people are currently infected with the virus in Austria.
The focus of the new infections is Vienna and Upper Austria.
Premier League: Man United and Chelsea qualified for Champions League
London (dpa) - English football record champions Manchester United return to the Champions League after a year's absence.
The Red Devils secured the third place on the last matchday of the Premier League by a 2:0 (0:0) -success against the direct competitor Leicester City.
The Foxes will now have to settle for the Europa League because Chelsea FC still passed and qualified for the premier class as the fourth.
In the fight against relegation, Aston Villa were allowed to cheer.
Manchester United took the lead through a penalty kick from Bruno Fernandes (71st minute).
After a red card for Leicester City in the aftermath, Jesse Lingard (90.+8) scored for the final score.
Chelsea, Bayern Munich's Champions League quarter-finals, won against Wolverhampton Wanderers in parallel with 2:0 (2:0) thanks to Mason Mount (45.+1) and Olivier Giroud (45.+4).
"That means a lot to me," said coach Frank Lampard, who in his first season as Chelsea coach is also in the FA Cup's final against Arsenal.
Tottenham Hotspur also benefited from Chelsea's victory against Wolverhampton, who still moved past the Wolves with a meager 1:1 (1:0) at Crystal Palace and starts sixth in the Europa League.
Aston Villa won the long-distance duel with FC Watford and AFC Bournemouth, but had to tremble for a long tide at West Ham United.
Jack Grealish's late lead (84.) immediately equalized Andrei Jarmolenko (85th).
The 1:1 (0:0) was enough because Watford lost with 2:3 (1:3) at Arsenal.
The club, which scuffed three coaches in the season, relegates after five first-league years.
Just like Bournemouth, who couldn't save himself through a 3:1 (2:1) at Everton.
In the end, a point was missing.
Liverpool FC, which has been English champions since the end of June, secured a club record with a 3:1 (1:1) victory at Newcastle United.
99 points is the highest the Reds have ever scored in a football season.
Coach Jürgen Klopp narrowly missed the points record in English football with his team.
Manchester City holds the record with 100 points in the 2017/18 season.
After the Newcastle lead in the first minute, Virgil van Dijk (38th), Divock Origi (59th) and Sadio Mané (89th) turned Sunday's game for Liverpool.
"For this wonderful club, it was an extraordinary season," goalscorer van Dijk said after the game, "that should spur us to defend the title.
It's going to be hard."
In the end, the Premier League bosses should also be satisfied.
Because of the coronavirus crisis, football operations in England had been disrupted from 13 March to 17 June.
In the meantime, there has even been discussion about a premature cancellation or hosting of the remaining games on neutral ground.
Finally, however, the season could be played to the end without special incidents - as in Germany without spectators in the stadiums.
Arrests and violence during demonstrations against Netanyahu
The protests in Israel against Prime Minister Netanyahu are becoming increasingly violent.
During the night there were violent clashes.
Israeli police have violently cracked down on Sunday night in Jerusalem against participants in a demonstration against right-wing Conservative Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu.
Twelve people have been arrested for participating in riots, police spokesman Micky Rosenfeld said.
Police deployed water cannons, among others, to dissolve the rally.
In total, according to media reports, around 6000 people had gathered near Netanyahu's official headquarters on Saturday night.
They protested against unemployment, corruption and Netanyahu's handling of the corona crisis.
Protesters slammed drums, blew into horns and called on Netanyahu to resign.
Participants of a counter-demonstration that supported Netanyahu were also arrested, the news site "ynet" reported.
Also in many other places in Israel, people demonstrated, including in front of the head of governmen's private mansion in the coastal city of Caesarea and in a
A man has been arrested near Tel Aviv, according to polices, because it is suspected to have sprayed pepper spray from a moving car on protesters on the side of the road.
Another man has been arrested following a knife attack on a protester in the south of the country.
The protester was slightly injured in the neck.
There have been several demonstrations against Netanyahu in Jerusalem and Tel Aviv.
The rallies accompany the corruption trial against the head of government.
Most recently, Netanyahu has also been criticised for its handling of the coronavirus crisis.
Among other things, he is accused of hasty easing and a lack of preparation for a second corona wave.
The coronavirus crisis has severely affected the country's economy.
The unemployment rate was more than 20 percent.
The number of new infections with the coronavirus in Israel has recently risen to new records.
Accident near Ahlen (NRW) - Vaner crashes on car - nine injured - Ruhrgebiet - Bild.de
Ahlen - A rear-end collision claimed nine lightly-injuries on the B58 at Ahlen on Saturday afternoon.
A female motorist was travelling with her two children (3, 5) in the direction of Beckum.
The police: "Probably she had suddenly braked because she wanted to turn".
The driver of a Mercedes van who drove behind her must probably have seen this too late.
He crashed into the car in the back.
Three occupants in the car and six in the van were slightly injured in the accident.
Several ambulances took her to a surrounding hospital.
Northoead coronavirus lockdown border city after coronavirus-suspected
North Korea has so far seen "luminous success" in its own coronavirus defense measures.
For the first time, the country is reporting on a suspected case.
Because of a first official coronavirus suspected case in the country, North Korea has completely sealed off a big city for fear of a disaster.
Despite the strictest epidemiological defensive measures in the past six months, "the vicious virus may have been introduced into the country," the state media reported.
For the region around the border town of Kaesong, the state of emergency has been imposed.
The communist leadership declared the highest alert level.
Should the suspected case be confirmed, it would be the first official infection case with Sars-CoV-2 in the foreclosed country.
North Korea has so far not registered a single infection and described its defensive measures as a "luminous success."
The data were doubted abroad.
The disease symptoms were reportedly detected in an "outlier" who illegally crossed the military demarcation line between South and North Korea on July 19.
The personant departed three years ago and has now returned across the border.
There was initially no confirmation from South Korea for crossing the border within the four-kilometre-wide and largely still mined military buffer zone.
South Korea's military, however, admitted the possibility of an illegal border crossing into North Korea as high, according to reports by the national news agency Yonhap.
There have been investigations into a number of people.
According to its own statements, North Korea convened an urgent meeting of the Politburo of the Workers' Party chaired by Kim Jong Un on Saturday because of the suspected case.
The "dangerous situation in Kaesong could trigger a deadly and destructive disaster."
Kim had already ordered on Friday as a pre-emptive measure "to completely block Kaesong and isolate each district and the region from others," it said.
The person concerned, as well as all people in Kaesong who had had contact with it, had been quarantined.
The party's central military commission is therefore to investigate the "outrager case" as well as the behavior of the responsible military unit and, if necessary, impose harsh punishment.
About 300,000 people live in Kaesong, according to estimates.
Tension before council meeting: City-Ost and noise protection are still up for discussion before big summer break
"In addition to environmental issues, the committee members will also vote on, among other things, the development plan on the large-scale downtown development-east project," it reads rather casually in the middle section of the municipal press release to the next municipal council meeting on Tuesday, July 28, at 4:30 p.m. in the Grand Hall of the CongressCentrum Pforzheim.
Above all, the appreciation of the eastern city is controversial until the end.
The majorities for this are scarce, most recently in individual committees, depending on attendance even dismissive.
An appeal from the economy (construction, real estate, commerce and IT), the project developed out of the citizenry and then by the administration and the local council not to fail after almost ten years of preparation, criticized project opponent Reinhard Klein as "extra-parliamentary opposition", "unsuspecting", "speculation", "vilification" and "deliberate false statements".
"Everyone thinks they know better," said Klein, who has been a member of the local council for a year as the only representative of the Pforzheim citizen list.
In fact, after reading the contracts, he is in this position: "I know what I'm talking about".
The phase-out is cheaper for the city, contrary to statements of the city administration after grants, of whose existence Klein is convinced to renovate for 8.5 million euros.
The city will develop very differently than one predicts in the town hall and on the part of the proponents from the economy.
Shaping the city through its own strength and not an investor: this is his plan B. One day after Klein, the FDP/FW/UB/LED Group, which also rejects the project, presented an alternative concept.
It essentially also provides for the renovation of the Technical Town Hall, the open keeping of the Schloßberg, the rental of the ex-thales for administrative areas and a more small-scale inner-city redesign ideally with local sponsors.
Environmental issues play a central role
In addition, environmental issues in particular will play a central role.
Among other things, the local council will vote on the noise action plan.
This includes various measures for the protection of the Pforzheim population against health noise pollution, caused by road traffic, including frequent speed reductions.
In a press release, the local Verkehrsclub Deutschland welcomes the widespread introduction of Tempo 30. However, the political leaders in the city are not innocent that this must happen, because they have contributed only to the massive increase in car traffic and the noise by neglecting the scheduled bus traffic with thinned cycles and through little commitment to cycling, according to the VCD.
The municipal council also votes on the participation of the city of the energy and climate protection agency Pforzheim/Enzkreis.
In addition, the agenda includes accession to the Climate Protection Pact of the State of Baden-Württemberg, the re-establishment of parental contributions in urban day-care facilities and the application for inclusion in the Smart City funding of the federal government.
Citizens are welcome, but must heed coronavirus protection measures.
Tim Zimmermann starts the new season with a lot of delay
With almost half a year's delay, Tim Zimmermann kicks off his premiere season in the ADAC GT Masters on July 31.
Before the league of super sports cars can start the regular operation, the first test drives were now scheduled.
For Zimmermann, it was mainly about getting to know his Lamborghini Huracan GT3 better.
The corona crisis has for a long time prevented Tim Zimmermann from embarking on his first business trip in the new Lamborghini Huracan from Grasser Racing.
Under special hygiene conditions, this was now possible.
The 23-year-old Langenargener made his first laps at the Lausitzring and got used to his premiere in the GT3 car.
"There are already a few things that are different than the Porsche from the pre-season," Zimmermann says.
"For example, you have to go into the curves differently with the ABS.
But it was a lot of fun.”
Zimmermann also had fun with Steijn Schothorst, with whom he will share his orange sports car in the GT Masters.
The Dutchman, despite his only 25 years, has been experienced in GT sports and has been able to help his new team-mate in many things.
"I'm not a beginner in motorsport, but every new series also brings new challenges," Zimmermann said.
"Steijn and I understand each other dazzlingly and we have also made enormous progress from session to session at the Lausitzring".
Right there - at the Lausitzring - the GT Masters will also host its first race.
From July 31, the 33 super sports cars will race through Lusatia.
Zimmermann, who is one of 15 newcomers, knows the track, however.
In 2015 he was most recently a guest at Motodrome.
"It worked quickly and I had little to get used to the track," says Zimmermann, who starts for the Friedrichshafen-based racing community Graf Zeppelin.
"The Lausitzring has a lot of fast passages, which you have to hit optimally in order to be fast".
How fast Zimmermann really is, that turns out in two weeks.
"Of course, the teams play poker a little during the tests," he said.
"In the end, however, we are very satisfied and looking forward to the first weekend at the Lausitzring".
The race in Brandenburg will be the prelude to a densely crowded calendar.
There will be seven races until the beginning of November.
What's standing at the moment is that no spectators will be allowed - but there's a celebrity guest.
In mid-August at the Nürburgring, ex-Formula 1 driver Niko Hülkenberg will reach into the GT steering wheel.
British transport minister caught cold by quarantine decision
The British government's decision to quarantine Spain holidaymakers for two weeks after their return has caught the country's transport minister cold, according to press reports.
Grant Shapps is currently on holiday in Spain, while his colleagues at home decided the measure at home on Saturday.
Due to the rising corona numbers in Spain, the cabinet in London removed the country from the list of those countries from which entry is possible without quarantine requirements.
The new rule will apply as of today, Sunday, so Shapps will also have to quarantine after his return.
In Austria, the government has also imposed quarantine measures for returnees.
But the regulation only applies to those who are just on holiday on the cut-off date, only from Saturday 1 August, and has not been introduced overnight.
Spain, by the way, has so far been considered one of the "safe countries" in Austria for which no quarantine is needed in Austria.
"Numerous ministers are likely to have known in advance that there is a possibility that a quarantine will be imposed on holidaymakers Spain," commented Labour MEP Diane Abbot.
"But apparently no one bothered to tell Grant Shapps."
Almost a month after the end of the corona emergency in Spain and the expiry of the strict curfew, infection numbers in the country are rising again.
Virologists mainly blame the nightlife for the renewed spread.
Spain is one of Britain's favourite holiday countries.
The British government advises against non-essential travel to the Spanish mainland at the moment, the Canaries and Balearic Islands, which are particularly popular with British holidaymakers.
So far, more than 272,000 infections with the novel coronavirus and more than 28,000 deaths have been recorded in Spain.
Russia: Navy Day: Putin Announces Hypersonic Weapons for Warships
Russian President Vladimir Putin has announced equipment of warships with state-of-the-art hypersonic weapons in his country.
With these weapons unique in the world, the combat possibilities of the Russian naval forces would be expanded, Putin said on Sunday in St. Petersburg at the central naval parade.
The Ministry of Defense also informed Interfax about successful tests of the zircon hypersonic missile built for the navy.
The hypersonic weapons have a multiple of the speed of sound of approximately 343 meters per second.
They are regarded as weapons of the future.
Unterwassertropedeo will continue to be built
It will also be built on the nuclear-embroiderable underwater torpedo Poseidon, it said.
The planned sea drone is planned to destroy coastal destinations.
The president, who himself was also riding a military boat, said that 40 new ships would be put into service at the individual fleet associations of Earth's largest country this year alone.
Russia has such a large coastal area than any other country in the world.
For 300 years, the navy has reliably protected Russia, Putin said at the day celebrated by sailors nationwide.
Also in many other Russian coastal towns, naval forces celebrated Navy Day with parades.
In Syria, where Russia fights alongside ruler Bashar al-Assad, the military demonstrated at an arms show in the Mediterranean port of Tartu's stronge.
Ammunition was also fired there.
In a video released by the Ministry of Defense, not only warships, including a submarine, but also combat helicopters and jets could be seen.
Hurricane "Hanna" reaches Texas: Help difficult because of corona
Washington - The first hurricane of this year's season over the Atlantic has come across land in Texas.
Life-threatening floods, strong winds and heavy rains now threatened parts of the U.S. state, the National Hurricane Center told the U.S. climate agency NOAA at night on Sunday (local time).
Gov. Greg Abbott declared a state of emergency for numerous counties.
The corona pandemic rampant in Texas is complicating the work of aid workers, Abbott said, according to U.S. media.
Evacuates called for caution
"Any hurricane is an enormous challenge," Abbott said, according to the Houston Chronicle newspaper.
Referring to the cyclone in Texas, he added: "This challenge is complicated and exacerbated, considering that it is sweeping through an area that is the state's worst-affected area by Covid-19."
According to Johns Hopkins University, around 5000 people have died so far in Texas alone as a result of the coronavirus pandemic.
Abbott urged people who had to leave their homes to exercise caution.
You shouldn't disregard practices "that we're used to, such as wearing masks," the governor said, according to CBS.
It is important for everyone to keep their distance.
Therefore, hotel rooms have also been provided in some places.
In addition, emergency shelters should be tested for corona.
Damage had initially only been reported from the coastal town of Port Mansfield.
Storm warning withdrawn again
With wind speeds of up to 150 kilometers per hour, "Hanna" had hit the island of Padre Island immediately off the Texas coast on land, according to authorities.
The tropical storm had previously been upgraded to hurricane's lowest level one.
For the coastal section from Port Mansfield to Sargent, a storm surge warning had been issued at first and later taken back again.
Authorities were expecting a slowdown, while "Hanna" moved further inland and headed towards the north-east of Mexico.
"Douglass" is moving towards Hawaii
In the Pacific, Hurricane "Douglas" was heading towards Hawaii at night to Sunday with wind speeds of around 150 kilometers per hour.
The hurricane center, however, expected it to weaken further.
According to the forecast, it is said to be close to the largest island of Hawaii on Sunday (local time) - then as a storm just below the hurricane threshold.
Over the Atlantic, NOAA expected an above-average hurricane season this year, according to a May forecast.
From June to the end of November, up to ten hurricanes are expected, of which up to six very strong hurricanes, the agency said.
On average, there are six hurricanes a year over the Atlantic, three of which develop into storms of great strength.
In view of available climate data and the higher water temperature in the Atlantic and the Caribbean, the probability of a normal season is only 30 percent, that one below-average even only ten percent, it said at the time.
U.S. President-elect Donald Trump tweeted that his administration was watching the storms closely and was in contact with the affected states.
Four-goals revoked: Video assistant "punished" Sassuolo
Sassuolo's Filip Djuricic were deprived of two goals
Naples - Four goals scored, four goals cancelled: Sassuolo Calcio experienced a raven-black evening at 0:2 (0:1) at the SSC Naples in the Italian football league Serie A - and was really mad at the video assistant of the referee.
"A festival of unconcerned gates for Sassuolo.
Never before has the VAR taken so hard action against a team," commented "Corriere dello Sport."
Host Naples had taken the lead through a goal from outside-back Elseid Hisaj in the 8th minute, in the 32nd minute Filip Djuricic initially hailed the equaliser.
The Video Assistant Referee (VAR) retracted the hit because of an offside position.
Five minutes later, Djuric shot the ball into the net again - the VAR intervened again.
Also the goals scored by attacker Francesco Caputo (50.) and Domenico Berardi (61.) took back the refereeing team after hinting from the VAR.
In post-match time, Allan made the 2:0 final score for Naples and the bitter evening for Sassuolo perfect.
"The VAR is worse for Sassuolo than a radar trap on the road and is punishing Sassuolo like no team before.
The new technology sets records," writes "Gazzetta dello Sport."
FC Bayern Munich: Pavard injured
Munich (dpa) - The start to the hot preparation phase for the Champions League began with a setback for double-winners FC Bayern.
Hansi Flick, who recovered "somewhat well" after a short holiday and a few days of training, will have to consider how he can rebuild his successful defense after the injury to world champion Benjamin Pavard.
The Frenchman, who has the third most minutes behind captain Manuel Neuer and midfield boss Joshua Kimmich, suffered a ligament injury to his leftroot and threatens to be missing in crucial king-class duels.
In Munich's Schmuddelwetter, the right-back broke off a training game and was driven off the course with a golf kart.
How long the unrestricted regular player has to pause in the fight for the title is open to question.
It is likely to be difficult to play in the eighth-round rematch against Chelsea on 8 August, the 24-year-old can hope for the final tournament starting in Lisbon on 12 August.
Bayern do not want to be stopped by the mishap from Sunday's training.
Flick, in his personal triple formula, cited the three attributes he wants to see from his stars on their way to Portugal's capital.
Quality, intensity and concentration are intended to show his victory-hungry title collectors in the ideally following 28 days until the king-class end game on 23 August.
"We mastered the obligatory tasks with championship and trophy just excellent.
Now it's time to create what's special," Flick said.
Until the Munich can deal with the final tournament and previously a training camp in the Algarve in concrete terms, however, is the eighth final duty against FC Chelsea.
After the 3:0 in the first leg before the corona break, the Londoners in the home arena should not once again become a Bayern nightmare as in the lost "Finale dahoam" 2012.
"We have to go through the door first.
We want to win, create good conditions and refuel confidence with a good game," Flick demanded.
A test to attune is on Friday against Olympique Marseille.
With a long address, Flick had sworn his stars, who had 13 days on leave after the double, to a once-in-a-lifetime August.
He welcomed 31 players at Säbener Strasse, including the new players led by Leroy Sané, who met all new colleagues on the pitch for the first time after his move from Manchester City.
"Leroy, like all the new ones, has blended in well," Flick reported.
But instead of talking about the new season, when Sané & Co. are no longer only allowed to train, Flick much rather talked about the current title project of the German series champion.
After days of individual and small-group training, team strength is now being trained for the Londoners, who, in contrast to the flick ensemble, are playing rhythms.
After that, a quarter-final against FC Barcelona and Lionel Messi could be imminent for the Munich side, in the semi-finances Real Madrid, Manchester City or Juventus Turin are possible opponents.
A path all the way to the final in Lisbon "isn't quite that easy," insisted the head coach, who can rely on the services of his new assistant Miroslav Klose in training from Tuesday.
The World Cup record goalscorer Klose and Flick know from many joint tournament preparations, what is particularly important in the 0-to-100 attunement.
With his current team, the work is "a pleasure," Flick praised.
"Focusedness on winnings" is an impressive trait of his team.
At the tournament with niner teams on Sunday, it was also ambitious to the cause.
Of the top performers, everyone was present, including the chumbling Thiago and defender David Alaba, whose whereabouts beyond the king-class seasoon is not secured.
Formula 1 needs a special rule to crown its world champion
Since it is not possible to drive on the required number of continents, the FIA has to improvise in order to award the World Championship titles.
However, all America races have been cancelled.
Race calendar is becoming more and more concrete
The racing calendar of the current Formula 1 season continues to take shape.
In addition to the ten confirmed Grand Prix during the week, there were three more race tracks.
In October it is planned to drive at the Nürburgring in Germany and Portimão in Portugal.
On the first of November, Lewis Hamilton and Co. compete for World Championship points on the traditional Italian race track in Imola.
According to the current status, the 2020 racing calendar thus comprises 13 races.
"We are pleased that we will continue to make great progress in completing our plans for the 2020 season," Formula One boss Chase Carey said.
A total of 15 to 18 races are to be completed in this extraordinary season in the premier class of motorsport.
Formula 1 wants to conclude its season in Asia.
The final race calendar is to be fixed and published in the coming weeks.
According to media reports, appearances in Vietnam and Malaysia could take place at the end of November ahead of the officially targeted races in Bahrain and Abu Dhabi in December.
Regulations call for races on three continents
The regulations of Formula 1 require at least races on three continents for the awarding of the world championship.
Even if, as currently planned, there is only run on two continents, there should be a world champion.
With the help of a special rule, the FIA wants to award the titles, citing "extraordinary circumstances" because of the coronavirus pandemic.
The traditional start of the season in Australia in March had to be cancelled at short notice due to the imminent coronavirus crisis.
A return to the continent later this year is not an issue.
In addition, Formula 1 will not be a guest in America this year.
Refurbition faces criticism in Brazil
Due to the tense corona situation, the races will not take place in Canada, the USA, Mexico and Brazil.
"We're looking forward to being back with them next season," Carey explained.
The cancellation of the American races met with criticism, especially in Brazil: "The cancellation did not come as a surprise to us, but we cannot accept the reasons for this," said Tamas Rohonyi, promoter of the Grand Prix in São Paulo.
For example, the number of infections in São Paulo is significantly lower than in other regions of Brazil.
Concerns raised by some teams about not wanting to drive in Brazil would also not justify a cancellation.
We are prepared for the required security measures.
Greta Thunberg: Bad eye against Donald Trump went around the world
Greta Thunberg spoke about her encounter with U.S. President Donald Trump.
Greta Thunberg was a guest on the well-known Latenight show of Stephen Colbert via video switch and revealed in the interview what she had in mind during her encounter with Donald Trump.
Climate activist Greta Thunberg was a guest on a popular U.S. Latenight show via video switch.
Among other things, she told about her fight against the powerful of the world.
The teenager also spoke about her famous "evil look" during her encounter with Donald Trump.
New York City - Over the past two years, climate activist Greta Thunberg attracted attention with her tireless commitment to the "Fridays For Future" movement.
Now, in a well-known late-night show, she talked about her plans in the future as well as her encounter with U.S. President Donald Trump at the U.N. climate summit last year.
Latenight presenter Stephen Colbert greeted 17-year-old Thunberg on your show on Tuesday and conducted a lengthy interview with the Swede.
In doing so, she spoke not only about her fight against climate change, but also about her conflict with the world’s powerful.
"There are a few things that surprised me," she said.
"How far people go in order to steer the focus away from the climate crisis," she shattered.
"Everything from organized hate campaigns to threats, just because a group of children takes to the streets," she said.
Heads of state and leading politicians who scoff against her on social media or even try to silence her find her "funny," as the reactions show her, "since they have no other answer to it."
It became particularly interesting at the end of the interview, when Greta Thunberg was approached about a well-known scene in which she gave US President Trump a bitter eye.
At the 2019 U.N. climate summit in New York, for which the activist sailed across the Atlantic, her expressive gaze was captured by the cameras and has since been seen by countless people on the internet.
From the short clip, a GIF was created, i.e. an animated image, which ultimately became one of the most commonly used images for Trump opponents.
But what did the 17-year-old think at that moment about the U.S. president?
In response to Stephen Colbert's relatively open question, she replied only briefly: "To be honest, I don't think that needs any further explanation."
The experienced satirist Colbert probably did not expect the quick-witted answer and briefly disappeared from the picture with a laugh.
With her "How dare you?" speech about the principles of climate protection, she was remembered by many at the climate conference.
Ex-Dortmunder Immobile with three-pack for Lazio Rome
Ciro Immobile is now on par with Bavaria's Robert Lewandowski in the race for Europe's Golden Shoe.
Former Dortmund's Ciro Immobile continues to have the best chance of becoming goalscoring king in the Italian football championship.
The former BVB professional contributed three goals in Lazio Rome's 5:1 (1:1) victory at Hellas Verona, including two converted penalties in the aftermath of both halves.
Thus, the 30-year-old from the fourth two games of the season before the end now has 34 season goals on his account - just as many Robert Lewandowski from Bayern Munich scored in the past Bundesliga season.
Cristiano Ronaldo has scored 30 goals so far in Italy, with the Portuguese superstar scoring Sampdoria Genoa in the evening.
Fleetwood Mac co-founder Peter Green has died
His life was like a roller coaster ride: after a fast-paced ascent with Fleetwood Mac, it went steeply downhill and then slowly back up again.
Blues rock guitarist Peter Green has died at the age of 73.
Ingenious guitarist, drug junkie, cult supporter: The co-founder of the band Fleetwood Mac, Peter Green, is dead.
He died peacefully in his sleep at the age of 73, family members said in an official statement on Saturday that another statement would be announced for the next few days.
Top musicians such as Peter Frampton paid tribute to Green as one of the best blues and rock guitarists.
Mick Fleetwood spoke in an emotional reaction of a "monumental loss" for all the past and present members of the band Fleetwood Mac.
Drummer Fleetwood and guitarist Green had founded the already legendary band in London in 1967, until today unforgotten his dreamy instrumental hit "Albatross".
The musician's life ("The End Of The Game," "In The Skies") resembled a roller coaster ride.
With Fleetwood Mac, Green experienced a fast-paced rise - the band celebrated their greatest successes but then without him.
Among other things, there is said to have been a dispute because Green wanted to donate all of the group's profits to charity.
He began performing in wide robes and with great crucifix.
Psychological problems are also said to have played a role.
Three years after its foundation, Green left the band in 1970.
This was followed by a social descent.
The blues-rock guitarist suffered from substance abuse and schizophrenia, according to biographers.
For a long time, Green was treated in psychiatry.
During a large part of the 1970s, the musician disappeared from the scene.
He is said to have temporarily lived on the streets, worked as a cemetery gardener and spent a time in an Israeli kibbutz.
Green was born in 1946 as the son of a Jewish working-class family in Bethnal Green.
From an early age, he was enthusiastic about U.S. blues musicians such as B.B. King and Muddy Waters.
At the age of eleven, he learned his first guitary grips.
At the age of 15, the teenager started playing guitar professionally and five years later was given the chance to stand on stage as a lead guitarist for the instrumental band Peter B's Looners.
A little later he was hired as a replacement for his idol Eric Clapton at the Bluesbreakers.
In the middle of the blues boom in the summer of 1967, Green founded Fleetwood Mac along with his Bluesbreakers colleagues Mick Fleetwood and John McVie.
Composed by Green, "Albatross" gave the group the first number 1 hit in the winter of 1968/69 and catapulted them to the top of the British music scene.
But the rapid ascension soon showed its downsides: Green could not cope with the media hype, his experiments with the drug LSD became more and more extreme.
He later commented on the life between music and addiction in an interview: "The worst thing that happened to me in my life are the drugs - the best thing was to end up a number 1 hit."
At the end of the decade, Green surprisingly achieved a success of respect in 1979 with an album inspired by religious themes.
Fleetwood Mac, meanwhile, swam on a wave of success without him in a new formation.
Her 1977 album "Rumours" sold more than 40 million copies worldwide.
Despite various small solo successes, Green did not make a major comeback until he relaunched in 1996 with a group of friendly musicians.
Britain's "Daily Telegraph" spoke at the time of a rock star returned to Earth.
Free from drugs, Green released some albums with the Peter Green Splinter Group until the band dissipated in 2004.
For music experts, he was already a blues legend during his lifetime - and with his soft sound one of the most influential guitarists of the past decades.
